course: dictionary
lesson: 2798
title: Disprove: showing an idea is false
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To disprove something means to show that it is false. You do this with facts, evidence, or one example that breaks the rule. «Benjamin Libet's experiments from the 1980s found brain activity before people felt they had decided on simple movements; many scientists and philosophers say they do not disprove free will.» Here the sentence says that the experiments did not show that free will was false.
explain: Scientists test an idea, called a hypothesis, with experiments. If the evidence goes against the idea, a good scientist changes their mind. In an argument, you try to disprove the other side's claims with evidence, not just by saying «No, you're wrong.»
explain: In maths, one counterexample is enough to disprove a rule. The rule «All odd numbers are prime» sounds true until you find 9. It is odd, but it is not prime, because 3 times 3 makes 9.
fact: To disprove means to show that something is false.
fact: You can disprove an idea with facts or evidence.
fact: One counterexample is enough to disprove a rule like «All odd numbers are prime».
fact: 9 is a counterexample: it is odd, but it is not prime.
fact: «Disproved» is the past form of disprove, and «disproven» is another past form.
exercise: «Many scientists say the experiments do not disprove free will.» What does disprove mean here? | To prove something is true | To forget something | To make something bigger | *To show that something is false
exercise: Which example would disprove the rule «All odd numbers are prime»? | *9, because it is odd and not prime | 3, because it is odd and prime | 11, because it is odd and prime | 2, because it is even and prime
exercise: A scientist's experiment shows that their idea is wrong. What should a good scientist do? | Ignore the evidence and keep the same idea | *Change their mind | Run the test once and forget it | Hide the results
test: Which word is the opposite of prove? | *Disprove | Remember | Hope | Guess
test: Why is 9 a useful example in maths? | Because it is even | Because it is the largest odd number | Because it is a prime number | *Because it breaks the rule that all odd numbers are prime
exam: Mia says, «My experiment proves my idea is right, so nothing can disprove it.» What is wrong with her thinking? | Scientists never change their minds | Only maths can be disproved | *Any idea can be tested, and new evidence could still disprove it | Experiments are never useful
